To implement a pull-based stream in Node.js, you can use the `Readable` stream class from the `stream` module. In a pull-based stream, the consumer requests data when it's ready to process it, rather than the stream pushing data to the consumer as it becomes available.

Here's a basic example of how to create a pull-based stream:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class MyPullBasedStream extends Readable {
  constructor(source) {
    super();
    this.source = source;
    this.index = 0;
  }

  _read(size) {
    if (this.index < this.source.length) {
      this.push(this.source[this.index]);
      this.index += 1;
    } else {
      this.push(null); // Signal the end of the stream
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const sourceData = ['item1', 'item2', 'item3'];
const myStream = new MyPullBasedStream(sourceData);

myStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log(chunk);
});

myStream.on('end', () => {
  console.log('No more data.');
});
```

In this example, the `MyPullBasedStream` class extends `Readable` and implements the `_read` method, which is called by the stream consumer when it's ready for more data. The `push` method is used to send data to the consumer, and pushing `null` signals the end of the stream.

